<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>A</m> is <m>({-2}, {-2})</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>({4}, {6})</m>. Give a point <m>P</m>, not <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>, with whole-number coordinates, such that angle <m>APB = 90^{\circ}</m>.</p><div><m>P = \big(</m><fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>, <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/><m>\big)</m>   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Angle <m>APB = 90^{\circ}</m> exactly when <m>P</m> is on the circle with diameter <m>AB</m> (the angle in a semicircle), apart from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Its centre is the midpoint <m>M({1}, {2})</m> and its radius is <m>\tfrac{1}{2}AB = {5}</m>.</p><p>So look for whole-number moves <m>(p, q)</m> from <m>M</m> with <m>p^2 + q^2 = {25}</m>. For example <m>({-3}, {5})</m> and <m>({4}, {-2})</m> both work.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>-3</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>